=== 1. is the junk really on the wire? ===

debug line -> sys.stderr
  server's first stdout line: {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"result":{"capabilities":{"experimental":{},"promp
  parses as a JSON-RPC frame

debug line -> sys.stdout
  server's first stdout line: [debug] index loaded, serving
  json.JSONDecodeError: Expecting value: line 1 column 2 (char 1)

=== 2. what does a real client do with it? ===

debug line -> sys.stderr  connected, 5 tools listed
                          0 parse errors logged
debug line -> sys.stdout  connected, 5 tools listed
                          1 parse errors logged

Read that twice. The client with a corrupted first line still connected.
It logged a parse failure, discarded the line, and carried on — so the
folklore version of this bug, 'one print breaks your server', is out of date
for this client. The junk is real; the client is tolerant of it.

Which makes the bug worse, not better. It is now silent.

=== 3. what happens to a print inside a tool ===

A tool that writes to stdout *while answering*, with no newline, so the
fragment should land immediately in front of the response frame:

  tools/call chatty -> tool returned 42
  parse errors logged: 0
  the server's stderr afterwards: '[debug] computing'

It worked, and the debug fragment came out on stderr — a stream the tool
never named. That is not luck. While it is serving, this SDK points file
descriptor 1 at stderr and descriptor 0 at the null device, so a stray write
from a handler, a library, or a child process physically cannot reach the
wire. It is restored when the server stops.

So both halves of the folklore are wrong for this SDK, in opposite
directions, and the true rule is narrower and more useful:

  * during serving   the SDK protects the wire for you
  * before serving   nothing does, and section 1 is what you get

Everything printed before run() is on the protocol: a module-level print, an
imported library's banner, a progress bar, a deprecation warning. That is the
window, and it is exactly where people put their debugging.

Do not rely on the protection either. It is this SDK, this version, this
transport; a client speaking to you over HTTP has no fd 1 to rescue. Give the
server a log() that writes to stderr, never allow a bare print() into the
file, and you are correct on every version of every transport.
